course: dictionary
lesson: 14258
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Neutralise is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. «An antacid neutralises the acid in your stomach.» Here the clue is acid. Mixing an acid with something else makes it neutral, so it is no longer harsh.
explain: «The team neutralised the other side's attack.» Now there is no acid at all. The clue is attack. To neutralise something here means to stop it from having an effect. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? Then try your meaning in the sentence, such as «stopped the attack», and check that it still makes sense.
explain: You do not need to know every meaning before you start. Read the sentence, look at the clue words, and pick the meaning that fits best.
fact: Neutralise is a verb, so it describes an action.
fact: Neutralise means to make an acid or alkali neutral by mixing them, or to stop something from having an effect.
fact: «An antacid neutralises stomach acid.» This is a science meaning, about acids and alkalis.
fact: «The goalkeeper neutralised the threat.» This meaning is about stopping something, not about chemicals.
fact: The forms are neutralise, neutralises, neutralised and neutralising. In American spelling it is neutralize.
exercise: «An antacid neutralises the acid in your stomach.» What does neutralises mean here? | Spreads the acid around the room | Heats the acid up until it boils | Makes the stomach bigger | *Makes the acid harmless by mixing it with something else
exercise: «The team neutralised the other side's attack.» What does neutralised mean? | Cheered the attack on | Copied the attack | Made the attack louder | *Stopped the attack from having an effect
exercise: Which form of the word is in «The chemist is neutralising the liquid»? | neutralised | neutralise | *neutralising | neutralises
test: «The goalkeeper neutralised the threat by catching the ball.» What does neutralised mean? | Kicked the ball out of the goal | Made the ball wet | *Stopped the threat from having an effect | Threw the threat away
test: Which way of checking a meaning is best? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Look only at the first letter | Guess and move on without reading the sentence | Count the letters in the word
exam: «Neutralise has more than one meaning. Which clue helps you pick the right one?» | *The words around it, such as acid or attack | Whether the word is written in capitals | The colour of the page | The number of letters in the word
